WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.101
Sometimes,

00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:17.208
you have a negative feeling about things.

00:00:18.708 --> 00:00:22.059
You're not happy
about the way things are going.

00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:25.083
You feel frustrated and dissatisfied,

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.750
and so often, we choose to live with it.

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:33.417
It's a negative that
we tell ourselves we have to endure.

00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:37.184
And yet, I passionately believe

00:00:37.208 --> 00:00:39.351
that we all have the ability

00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:41.643
to turn that negative feeling

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.476
into a positive

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.351
by allowing our dissatisfaction

00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:48.042
to give birth to change.

00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.601
On January 6, 1999,

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:54.393
I was working in London

00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:56.851
when the news channels began to report

00:00:56.875 --> 00:01:00.143
the rebel invasion of my hometown,

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:02.208
Freetown, Sierra Leone.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.893
Thousands of people lost their lives,

00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:09.351
and there were bodies
littering the streets of Freetown.

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:13.434
My husband's elderly aunt
was burned alive,

00:01:13.458 --> 00:01:16.434
and I thought of my own two-year old

00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:19.875
as I saw images of little children
with amputated limbs.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.809
Colleagues said to me,

00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:24.726
"How could we help?"

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.601
I didn't know,

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:30.893
so I began to call the telephone numbers
that came up on my screen

00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:34.434
as international aid agencies
started to make appeals

00:01:34.458 --> 00:01:37.458
to raise money to address the tragedy.

00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:43.393
The vagueness of those telephone
conversations disappointed me.

00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:46.059
It felt like the people
who were raising the money

00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:48.559
seemed so far removed from the crisis,

00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:50.851
and understandably so,

00:01:50.875 --> 00:01:52.559
but I wasn't satisfied

00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:54.393
and I wasn't convinced

00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:58.018
that the interventions
they would eventually implement

00:01:58.042 --> 00:02:01.250
would actually have the level of impact
that was so clearly needed.

00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:05.018
There were butterflies
in my stomach for days

00:02:05.042 --> 00:02:08.726
as I continued to watch
horrors unfold on television,

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.309
and I continuously asked myself,

00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:13.143
what could I be doing?

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.934
What should I be doing?

00:02:14.958 --> 00:02:18.851
What I wanted to do was to help
children affected by the war.

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:20.125
So that's what we did.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.559
Myself, my sister and some friends

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:28.601
started the Sierra Leone
War Trust For Children, SLWT.

00:02:28.625 --> 00:02:32.059
We decided to focus
on the thousands of displaced people

00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:33.809
that fled the fighting

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:37.351
and were now living
in really poor, difficult conditions

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:39.518
in camps in Freetown.

00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:42.351
Our work started with the Ross Road Camp

00:02:42.375 --> 00:02:44.434
at the east end of the city.

00:02:44.458 --> 00:02:47.268
Working with a local health organization,

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:52.434
we identified about 130
of the most vulnerable single mothers

00:02:52.458 --> 00:02:55.059
with children under the age of five,

00:02:55.083 --> 00:03:00.476
supporting them
by providing business skills,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.434
microcredit,

00:03:02.458 --> 00:03:04.684
whatever they asked us.

00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:07.476
Working in those difficult conditions,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.184
just getting the basics right,
was no small task,

00:03:11.208 --> 00:03:14.351
but our collective sense
of dissatisfaction

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:17.018
at an unacceptable status quo

00:03:17.042 --> 00:03:19.875
kept us focused on getting things done.

00:03:20.958 --> 00:03:24.434
Some of those women went on
to open small businesses,

00:03:24.458 --> 00:03:26.101
repaid their loans

00:03:26.125 --> 00:03:28.809
and allowed other mothers
and their children

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:31.726
to have the same opportunity they did.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.559
And we, we kept on going.

00:03:34.583 --> 00:03:38.726
In 2004, we opened
an agricultural training center

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.601
for ex-child soldiers,

00:03:40.625 --> 00:03:42.643
and when the war was behind us,

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:46.559
we started a scholarship program
for disadvantaged girls

00:03:46.583 --> 00:03:50.083
who would otherwise not be able
to continue in school.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.059
Today, Stella, one of those girls,

00:03:54.083 --> 00:03:56.851
is about to qualify as a medical doctor.

00:03:56.875 --> 00:04:01.018
It's amazing what a dose
of dissatisfaction can birth.

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:03.601
(Applause)

00:04:03.625 --> 00:04:06.559
Ten years later, in 2014,

00:04:06.583 --> 00:04:08.893
Sierra Leone was struck by Ebola.

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:15.143
I was working in Freetown at the time
on a hotel construction project on May 25

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.351
when the first cases were announced,

00:04:17.375 --> 00:04:20.809
but I was back in London on July 30

00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:24.143
when the state of emergency was announced,

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:29.208
the same day that many airlines
stopped their flights to Sierra Leone.

00:04:30.292 --> 00:04:32.684
I remember crying for hours,

00:04:32.708 --> 00:04:36.208
asking God, why this? Why us?

00:04:37.792 --> 00:04:39.643
But beyond the tears,

00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:42.018
I began to feel again

00:04:42.042 --> 00:04:45.101
that profound sense of dissatisfaction.

00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:49.351
So when, six months after
those first cases had been confirmed,

00:04:49.375 --> 00:04:52.851
the disease was still spreading
rapidly in Sierra Leone

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:57.518
and the number of people
infected and dying continued to rise,

00:04:57.542 --> 00:04:59.351
my level of frustration and anger

00:04:59.375 --> 00:05:03.309
got so much that I knew I could not stay

00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:06.167
and watch the crisis
from outside Sierra Leone.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.934
So, in mid-November,

00:05:09.958 --> 00:05:12.768
I said goodbye to my much loved

00:05:12.792 --> 00:05:15.809
and very understanding
husband and children,

00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:17.976
and boarded a rather empty plane

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.768
to Freetown.

00:05:19.792 --> 00:05:23.143
Freetown was now
the epicenter of the outbreak.

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:26.476
There were hundreds
of new cases every week.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.268
I spoke to many medical experts,

00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:30.809
epidemiologists

00:05:30.833 --> 00:05:33.018
and ordinary people every day.

00:05:33.042 --> 00:05:34.667
Everyone was really scared.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:41.143
"We won't succeed until we're talking
to people under the mango tree."

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:43.018
So said Dr. Yoti,

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:46.684
a Ugandan doctor who worked for WHO

00:05:46.708 --> 00:05:49.643
and who had been involved
in pretty much every Ebola outbreak

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:51.559
in Africa previously.

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:52.893
He was right,

00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:56.125
and yet there was no plan
to make that happen.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.393
So during a weekend in early December,

00:06:00.417 --> 00:06:05.434
I developed a plan that became known
as the Western Area Surge plan.

00:06:05.458 --> 00:06:08.351
We needed to talk with people,

00:06:08.375 --> 00:06:10.059
not at people.

00:06:10.083 --> 00:06:14.143
We needed to work
with the community influencers

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:16.518
so people believed our message.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:19.018
We needed to be talking
under the mango tree,

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:20.893
not through loudspeakers.

00:06:20.917 --> 00:06:22.208
And we needed more beds.

00:06:22.917 --> 00:06:25.851
The National Ebola Response Center, NERC,

00:06:25.875 --> 00:06:28.643
built on and implemented that plan,

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.601
and by the third week of January,

00:06:30.625 --> 00:06:33.934
the number of cases
had fallen dramatically.

00:06:33.958 --> 00:06:35.351
I was asked to serve

00:06:35.375 --> 00:06:38.059
as a new Director of Planning for NERC,

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:40.893
which took me right across the country,

00:06:40.917 --> 00:06:42.976
trying to stay ahead of the outbreak

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.643
but also following it

00:06:44.667 --> 00:06:46.893
to remote villages in the provinces

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:50.601
as well as to urban slum communities.

00:06:50.625 --> 00:06:53.684
On one occasion, I got out of my car

00:06:53.708 --> 00:06:56.417
to call for help for a man
who had collapsed on the road.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:07:00.143
I accidentally stepped in liquid

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:02.976
that was coming down the road
from where he lay.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.726
I rushed to my parents' house,

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.101
washed my feet in chlorine.

00:07:07.125 --> 00:07:11.559
I'll never forget waiting
for that man's test results

00:07:11.583 --> 00:07:16.250
as I constantly checked my temperature
then and throughout the outbreak.

00:07:17.292 --> 00:07:22.101
The Ebola fight was probably
the most challenging

00:07:22.125 --> 00:07:25.018
but rewarding experience of my life,

00:07:25.042 --> 00:07:26.976
and I'm really grateful

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.601
for the dissatisfaction

00:07:28.625 --> 00:07:30.643
that opened up the space

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:32.083
for me to serve.

00:07:32.833 --> 00:07:37.351
Dissatisfaction can be
a constant presence in the background,

00:07:37.375 --> 00:07:38.809
or it can be sudden,

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:40.500
triggered by events.

00:07:41.667 --> 00:07:42.917
Sometimes it's both.

00:07:44.125 --> 00:07:46.958
With my hometown, that's the way it was.

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:51.708
For years, our city had changed,

00:07:52.417 --> 00:07:54.625
and it had caused me great pain.

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:57.351
I remember a childhood

00:07:57.375 --> 00:07:59.792
growing up climbing trees,

00:08:00.708 --> 00:08:03.684
picking mangoes and plums

00:08:03.708 --> 00:08:06.667
on the university campus
where my father was a lecturer.

00:08:07.958 --> 00:08:12.309
Went fishing in the streams
deep in the botanical gardens.

00:08:12.333 --> 00:08:18.559
The hillsides around Freetown
were covered with lush green vegetation,

00:08:18.583 --> 00:08:20.893
and the beaches were clean and pristine.

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:25.476
The doubling of the population of Freetown
in the years that followed the civil war,

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.851
and the lack of planning
and building control

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.458
resulted in massive deforestation.

00:08:32.125 --> 00:08:36.851
The trees, the natural beauty,
were destroyed as space was made

00:08:36.875 --> 00:08:39.559
for new communities, formal or informal,

00:08:39.583 --> 00:08:42.351
and for the cutting down of firewood.

00:08:42.375 --> 00:08:45.042
I was deeply troubled and dissatisfied.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.684
It wasn't just the destruction
of the trees and the hillsides

00:08:49.708 --> 00:08:51.018
that bothered me.

00:08:51.042 --> 00:08:54.268
It was also the impact of people,

00:08:54.292 --> 00:08:59.309
as infrastructure failed to keep up
with the growth of the population:

00:08:59.333 --> 00:09:02.393
no sanitation systems to speak of,

00:09:02.417 --> 00:09:06.625
a dirty city with typhoid,
malaria and dysentery.

00:09:07.375 --> 00:09:09.768
I didn't know the statistics at the time,

00:09:09.792 --> 00:09:12.851
but it turned out that by 2017,

00:09:12.875 --> 00:09:18.059
only six percent of liquid waste
and 21 percent of solid waste

00:09:18.083 --> 00:09:19.393
was being collected.

00:09:19.417 --> 00:09:21.518
The rest was right there with us,

00:09:21.542 --> 00:09:25.226
in backyards, in fields, rivers

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.500
and deposited in the sea.

00:09:28.708 --> 00:09:33.809
The steps to address that deep sense
of anger and frustration I felt

00:09:33.833 --> 00:09:36.768
didn't unfold magically or clearly.

00:09:36.792 --> 00:09:40.643
That's not how the power
of dissatisfaction works.

00:09:40.667 --> 00:09:44.226
It works when you know
that things can be done better,

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:49.184
and it works when you decide to take
the risks to bring about that change.

00:09:49.208 --> 00:09:52.518
And so it was that in 2017

00:09:52.542 --> 00:09:54.851
I ended up running for mayor,

00:09:54.875 --> 00:09:56.833
because I knew things could be better.

00:09:57.458 --> 00:10:01.184
It seemed the people agreed with me,
because I won the election.

00:10:01.208 --> 00:10:04.184
(Applause)

00:10:04.208 --> 00:10:08.684
Today, we are implementing
an ambitious plan

00:10:08.708 --> 00:10:10.792
to transform our city,

00:10:11.917 --> 00:10:14.518
and when I say we,

00:10:14.542 --> 00:10:16.351
what gets me really excited

00:10:16.375 --> 00:10:19.643
is that I mean
the whole Freetown community,

00:10:19.667 --> 00:10:25.851
whether it's being part of competitions
like rewarding the neighborhood

00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:29.309
that makes the most improvement
in overall cleanliness,

00:10:29.333 --> 00:10:31.059
or whether it's our programs

00:10:31.083 --> 00:10:35.809
that are leading and joining
people and waste collectors

00:10:35.833 --> 00:10:37.417
through our apps.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.184
In Freetown today,

00:10:41.208 --> 00:10:43.042
it's a much cleaner city,

00:10:44.208 --> 00:10:47.226
and those trees
that we're so well known for,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:51.018
we planted 23,000 of them
last rainy season.

00:10:51.042 --> 00:10:52.309
(Applause)

00:10:52.333 --> 00:10:53.643
And in 2020,

00:10:53.667 --> 00:11:00.101
we plan to plant a million trees as part
of our "Freetown the Tree Town" campaign.

00:11:00.125 --> 00:11:03.226
(Applause)

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:08.042
Sometimes, sometimes we have
a negative feeling about things.

00:11:08.917 --> 00:11:12.101
We're not happy about
the way things are going.

00:11:12.125 --> 00:11:14.018
We feel dissatisfied,

00:11:14.042 --> 00:11:15.292
and we feel frustrated.

00:11:16.708 --> 00:11:19.667
We can change that negative
into a positive.

00:11:22.083 --> 00:11:26.101
If you believe that things can be better,

00:11:26.125 --> 00:11:31.809
then you have the option to do something
rather than to do nothing.

00:11:31.833 --> 00:11:36.143
The scale and circumstances
of our situations will differ,

00:11:36.167 --> 00:11:37.542
but for each of us,

00:11:38.958 --> 00:11:41.625
we all have one thing in common.

00:11:42.917 --> 00:11:45.893
We can take risks to make a difference,

00:11:45.917 --> 00:11:48.059
and I will close in saying,

00:11:48.083 --> 00:11:49.601
step out,

00:11:49.625 --> 00:11:50.934
take a risk.

00:11:50.958 --> 00:11:54.184
If we can unite behind
the power of dissatisfaction,

00:11:54.208 --> 00:11:55.976
the world will be a better place.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.726
Thank you.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.375
(Applause)

